{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"24580429-869c-4715-aff9-d5b01a1d6cc0","status":"SUCCESS","response":"Autonomous Integrator online. Context loaded. Monitoring `VERDICT.md`.\n\n### Available Tools\nHere are the exact names of every tool declared in the environment:\n\n1. `view_file`\n2. `write_to_file`\n3. `replace_file_content`\n4. `run_command`\n5. `manage_task`\n6. `schedule`\n7. `invoke_subagent`\n8. `define_subagent`\n9. `manage_subagents`\n10. `send_message`\n11. `ask_question`\n12. `finish`\n13. `read_url_content`\n14. `search_web`\n15. `list_resources`\n16. `read_resource`\n17. `mcp_gemini-api_gemini-api-docs_gemini_search_docs`\n18. `mcp_gemini-api_gemini-api-docs_gemini_get_doc`\n\n---\n\n### Testable Hypothesis\n\n\u003e **Hypothesis:** The daytime sky appears blue because atmospheric gas molecules scatter shorter wavelengths of incoming solar light (such as blue and violet light) with significantly greater efficiency than longer wavelengths (such as red and yellow light), in accordance with Rayleigh scattering ($I \\propto \\lambda^{-4}$).\n\u003e\n\u003e **Test Method:** Measure the spectral intensity and polarization of scattered diffuse daylight across the visible spectrum (380–750 nm) at varying zenith angles and solar elevations using an optical spectrometer, and compare the observed spectral radiance slope against theoretical Rayleigh predictions and baseline measurements taken at varying atmospheric pressures/altitudes.\n\n---\n\nWorkspace state has shifted back to the Principal Investigator (Claude Fable 5.1) for strategic review.\n{\"hypothesis\":\"The daytime sky appears blue because atmospheric gas molecules scatter shorter wavelengths of incoming solar light (such as blue and violet light) with significantly greater efficiency than longer wavelengths (such as red and yellow light), in accordance with Rayleigh scattering (I ∝ λ^-4).\",\"toolAction\":\"Completing task\",\"toolSummary\":\"Task completion\",\"tools\":[\"view_file\",\"write_to_file\",\"replace_file_content\",\"run_command\",\"manage_task\",\"schedule\",\"invoke_subagent\",\"define_subagent\",\"manage_subagents\",\"send_message\",\"ask_question\",\"finish\",\"read_url_content\",\"search_web\",\"list_resources\",\"read_resource\",\"mcp_gemini-api_gemini-api-docs_gemini_search_docs\",\"mcp_gemini-api_gemini-api-docs_gemini_get_doc\"]}\n","duration_seconds":10.529125,"num_turns":2,"structured_output":{"hypothesis":"The daytime sky appears blue because atmospheric gas molecules scatter shorter wavelengths of incoming solar light (such as blue and violet light) with significantly greater efficiency than longer wavelengths (such as red and yellow light), in accordance with Rayleigh scattering (I ∝ λ^-4).","tools":["view_file","write_to_file","replace_file_content","run_command","manage_task","schedule","invoke_subagent","define_subagent","manage_subagents","send_message","ask_question","finish","read_url_content","search_web","list_resources","read_resource","mcp_gemini-api_gemini-api-docs_gemini_search_docs","mcp_gemini-api_gemini-api-docs_gemini_get_doc"]},"json_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"tools":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"hypothesis":{"type":"string"}},"required":["tools","hypothesis"],"additionalProperties":false},"usage":{"input_tokens":58196,"output_tokens":618,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":0,"total_tokens":58814}}}
